course: dictionary
lesson: 1960
title: Comparison
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A comparison is when you look at two things to see how they are alike or different. You can also use the word to say one thing is like another: «She is as brave as a lion» puts a person next to a lion, so you can picture how brave she is.
explain: In maths, a comparison shows whether one amount is more than, less than or equal to another. The signs > (greater than), < (less than) and = (equal to) are comparisons. Find the two amounts first, then decide which one is bigger, smaller or the same.
explain: Phrases such as «in comparison» and «by comparison» use the same idea: you put one thing beside another to judge them. When you read a comparison, ask yourself: what is being compared, and what is it being compared with?
fact: A comparison looks at two things to see how they are alike or different.
fact: «Good, better, best» and «bad, worse, worst» are irregular comparisons, so they do not follow the usual pattern.
fact: Sometimes a part is told by comparison, as in «10 more than the first»: find the first part before you work out the answer.
fact: A false comparison is when you think you can do something just because someone else can; «Someone else being able to do something does not mean you can.»
fact: «Wicked» (Boston and New England) means very, so «wicked good» means very good; in Liverpool a «like» at the end of a sentence is a filler word, not a comparison.
exercise: In «She is as brave as a lion», what two things are being compared? | two cats | *a person and a lion | a lion and a tiger | the sun and the moon
exercise: Which sign shows that 7 is greater than 4? | *> | < | =
exercise: The first score is 10, and the second is «10 more than the first». What is the second score? | *20 | 10 | 9 | 100
test: Which word is an irregular comparison of «bad»? | *worse | badder | bigger | good
test: In Boston, what does «wicked good» mean? | good in a wicked way | very bad | *very good | not very good
exam: Which one is a false comparison? | *Ella can swim, so I can too | Ella is taller than Sam | Ella is 9 and Sam is 10 | Ella and Sam both like football
